welcome to europe's latest headache a  fault line where the european union ends  and a sliver of russia begins  here to the southwest of lithuania is  kaliningrad it's a russian legacy of the  second world war an exclave about the  size of northern ireland  for decades it's been supplied by cargo  running through lithuania but now  sanctions have been imposed on things  like building materials and coal  and these trains are arriving half empty  russia has threatened reprisals and  lithuania's government is nervous  there are discussions with the western  allies about a permanent nato brigade in  lithuania this situation shows that we  really need to have additional  capabilities in lithuania  the unanswered question lingers just  what is russia threatening standing here  you can see why lithuania is so nervous  russia has stationed short and  long-range missiles and built a mighty  military base right on the border of the  european union  not everyone is anxious though virgilius  and thomas grew up here they talk of  kaliningrad as a neighbour  we have friends in there in russia there  are normal peoples i think  and they are not so dangerous you know  but as you move further away opinions  harden  arena's parents brought her from russia  to lithuania when she was just a child  she says she grieves for her home  country and worries that russia might  now attack  i am afraid i feel angry because the war  has all people here in the vienna it's  normal thinking what's happening in  ukraine right now so just thinking  always we remember always about this  in moscow broadcasters call this all a  blockade  russia's media has been increasingly  controlled since the war in ukraine  began something else lithuania says it's  trying to address  so here's the overnight passenger train  from moscow pulling across the  lithuanian border  it stops for customs checks  once passengers would have got out to  stretch their legs now they all stay on  board  it is unclear how much they know about  the war  along this platform there are huge  posters showing graphic images accusing  russia of genocide  behind the curtains of the train  passengers are invited to use free wi-fi  but first they have to scroll through  more photos from the war  the train sets off slowly towards  kaliningrad this is a story about  sanctions and borders but it's shaped by  conflict and a fracturing continent  adam parsons sky news lithuania
 

